---
title: "Taliesin"
description: "The user guide: how to write, preview, execute and publish a .tmd document, from your first preview to a deployed site."
---

# Why Taliesin {.unnumbered}

**A fast, single-purpose dev server that turns `.tmd` files into live HTML (blog
posts, papers, books, and whole websites) with executable code blocks and an
editing loop that updates the instant you save.** It reads the Markdown/Pandoc
syntax you already know, and makes one trade: do one thing, author HTML, and make
the edit loop instant.

## Three things it gets right

- **Click-to-source.** Every rendered block remembers the line it came from.
  Ctrl-click (Cmd-click on Mac) anything in the preview to jump straight to its
  source, even across `{{< include >}}`d files.
- **Block-level incremental updates.** A save re-renders only the blocks that
  changed. Live canvases (Three.js, `{js}` cells) keep their state; the
  page never reloads.
- **No per-edit startup cost.** A warm server and a warm Jupyter kernel mean
  edits show up immediately, and only the cells whose code actually changed
  re-run.

That loop is the whole product; everything below is what you can put inside it.

This guide is itself a multi-page **book** built with Taliesin: the Chapters drawer, search
(press <kbd>Cmd/Ctrl-K</kbd>), live diagrams, and executed code cells included. A
companion book, [Internals](https://internals.taliesin.sh/), covers how it works inside,
and the [Gallery](https://gallery.taliesin.sh/) has whole projects you can open and read.

## Get running

```sh
taliesin preview post.tmd 4321   # live preview, updating as you save
taliesin build post.tmd          # one self-contained HTML file, code cells executed
taliesin preview blog/           # a directory instead: a whole multi-page site
```

Open `http://127.0.0.1:4321`, edit, and watch a single block update in place with your
scroll preserved. The server binds to loopback only. [Getting
started](using/getting-started.tmd) installs the binary and sets up a kernel.

## What it is (and isn't)

::: {.callout-note}
## Scope

Taliesin renders to **HTML only**: there is no PDF path and no format matrix. It
is one author's writing workflow, kept small and fast on purpose. [Choosing
Taliesin](using/choosing.tmd) is the chapter on what that buys and what it costs.
:::

Start with [Getting started](using/getting-started.tmd). The rest of this guide
tours what you can put on a page: [the live preview loop](using/preview.tmd), [executable
content](using/code.tmd) (Python cells), [interactive
content](using/interactive.tmd) (`{js}` cells, 3D graphics), [rich
writing](using/writing.tmd) (math, diagrams, figures, citations), and [recipes for
books and sites](using/recipes.tmd).
